  "worked_solution": "Let $R$ be the event that the selected card is red, and let $A$ be the event that the card was one of the cards of deck $A.$ Then ${A}^{\u2032}$ is the event that the card was one of the cards of deck $B.$\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected card is red, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\nP(R)=P(R\\cap A)+P(R\\cap {A}^{\u2032})\n$$\n\nTo calculate $P(R\\cap A),$ we take the number of red cards in deck $A$ $(26)$ and divide by the total number of cards in both decks $(52+50=102).$ So, we have\n\n$$\nP(R\\cap A)=\\frac{26}{102}.\n$$\n\nSimilarly, to calculate $P(R\\cap {A}^{\u2032}),$ we take the number of red cards in deck $B$ $(24)$ and divide by the total number of cards in both decks $(102).$ So, we have\n\n$$\nP(R\\cap {A}^{\u2032})=\\frac{24}{102}.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the above information into the law of total probability, the probability that a randomly selected card from either deck is red is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(R) & =P(R\\cap A)+P(R\\cap {A}^{\u2032}) \\\\  & =\\frac{26}{102}+\\frac{24}{102} \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{51}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
